<DIV align="left" style="margin-left: 0%; margin-right: 0%; text-indent: 4%; font-size: 10pt; font-family: 'Times New Roman', Times; color: #000000; background: #FFFFFF">
    <B><I>Contraction of the banking system and solvency issues
    which have greatly diminished the capacity of major banks to
    provide commercial mortgage loans and credit facilities to
    property owners.</I></B>&#160;&#160;Commercial banks are
    estimated by the Commercial Mortgage Securities Association to
    hold $1.5&#160;trillion, or 43%, of all commercial mortgages.
    The primary driver for banks holding so many commercial
    mortgages was the securitization market, which allowed banks to
    liquidate loans held on balance sheet into off-balance sheet
    vehicles. The larger commercial and investment banks have
    suffered significant losses and erosion of capital reserves over
    the past year due in large part to their holdings in real estate
    structured finance vehicles as well as direct holdings in
    subprime, residential and commercial real estate loans. The
    banking industry has been transformed over the past year by
    bankruptcies, business combinations and capital infusions from
    the U.S.&#160;Treasury. As noted above, the market for CMBS and
    commercial real estate CDO issuance has closed, leading to the
    elimination of credit facilities provided by banks as a bridge
    to securitization. In addition, according to a January 2009
    Senior Loan Officer Opinion Survey on Bank Lending Practices,
    more than 80% of the surveyed lenders had significantly
    tightened their lending policies translating to reduced
    loan-to-value ratios and wider loan-rate spreads. As a result of
    this tightening and liquidity constraints, the Mortgage Bankers
    Association reports that commercial and multifamily loan
    originations dropped by 86% during 2008 when compared to the
    prior year.
</DIV>

