<DIV align="left" style="margin-left: 0%; margin-right: 0%; text-indent: 4%; font-size: 10pt; font-family: 'Times New Roman', Times; color: #000000; background: #FFFFFF">
    According to the Mortgage Bankers Association, a significant
    share of the CMBS loans maturing in the next two years are
    floating rate or bridge loans, which tend to be shorter term and
    have larger balances than fixed rate commercial loans. As a
    result of declining valuations, triggered in large part by the
    absence of available sources of funding for buyers as well as
    declining operating performance in many segments of the economy,
    repayment through a sale of assets is not a viable option for
    many property owners. To date, government assistance has been
    limited to a prospective plan to expand the Term Asset-Backed
    Securities Loan Facility, or TALF, to include as eligible
    collateral for loans made by the Federal Reserve Bank of New
    York under TALF, secured and unsecured loans on commercial real
    estate assets that have a long-term credit rating in the highest
    investment grade rating category (AAA). While we believe that
    such a credit facility would provide some added capacity to
    address the enormous credit shortfall facing commercial real
    estate, we also believe that there will continue to be
    insufficient capacity in the system to refinance maturing,
    performing commercial real estate loans. Due to limited
    competition from a scarcity of debt capital, we believe that
    these market conditions should allow lenders such as us to be
    very selective in making their investments, to apply stringent
    underwriting criteria and enhanced structural features in the
    loans they originate (e.g., personal recourse requirements and
    cross collateralization) and to increase fees and pricing,
    particularly for more subordinate layers of the capital
    structure.
</DIV>

